TRIMMING A TRAILING BLANK LINE IS CORRECT, AND IT SETTLES IN ONE PASS.

`then-args` runs `format` again over the first run's output, so `expected.md`
is the SECOND pass. One pass reaches the answer and the next changes nothing.

WHY THIS CASE EXISTS. It records a conclusion, and the conclusion is that
there is NO defect here. While chasing an empty list item that `format`
deletes, the run looked damning:

    "# Title\n\n- \n"  ->  "# Title\n\n"  ->  "# Title\n"

Three different documents, so `format` appeared not to settle. The reading was
wrong. The blank line in the middle answer is not a formatting mistake that a
later pass repairs; it is the hole left by the DELETED ITEM, and deleting the
item is the whole of the defect. See `format-keeps-an-empty-list-item`.

This case isolates the normalization from the deletion by removing the list.
Trimming the trailing blank line is right, and it is a fixed point, so it can
be ruled out the next time a run of `format` seems unstable. Blaming the
normalization would have sent the fix into `BlockFormatter`, which is nowhere
near the guard in `MarkdownParser.collectListItems` that actually drops the
item.

A NOTE ON WHAT COUNTS AS SETTLED. The bar is that a SECOND pass changes
nothing, not that the first pass changes nothing. The first pass is the
normalization and it is expected to move things. What must never happen is a
document that keeps moving, which is what
`format-formats-a-split-item-the-same-way-twice` guards for lists.
